# Python Development Standards

> file_patterns: ["*.py"]
> description: Core principles and standards for Python development

## Core Principles

### KISS Principle
Keep It Simple Stupid

- Write the simplest possible code
- Avoid over-design
- Keep code easy to understand and maintain
- Ensure code is easily comprehensible for others and AI

### Single Responsibility
Each component should have a single, well-defined purpose

- One file per module
- Each module should have minimal functionality
- Clear responsibility for each class/function

### Incremental Development
Develop code incrementally with small iterations

- Small iterative steps
- Build framework first, then details
- Maintain testability

## Code Quality

### Type Annotations
> required: true

- Use type hints for all function parameters
- Use type hints for all return values
- Use Optional[] for nullable values
- Use Union[] for multiple types
- Keep type hints simple and readable

### Documentation
> required: true

- Include purpose description
- Document parameters and return values
- Include usage examples where helpful
- Document exceptions and edge cases
- Keep existing comments up to date

### Code Style
> required: true

- Follow PEP 8 conventions
- Use consistent naming conventions
- Maintain proper indentation
- Keep line lengths reasonable

## Data Processing

### Data Structures
Use dataclass for data structure definitions

- Use dataclasses for data containers
- Include type hints for all fields
- Use frozen=True for immutable data
- Consider slots=True for performance

### Data Validation
Use pydantic for data validation

- Define clear validation rules
- Use appropriate field types
- Include error messages
- Handle validation errors gracefully

### Caching
Use lru_cache for computation results

- Cache expensive computations
- Set appropriate cache sizes
- Consider TTL for time-sensitive data
- Clear cache when needed

## Error Handling

### Async Processing
Use async/await for asynchronous operations

- Use async for I/O operations
- Handle concurrent operations properly
- Use appropriate async patterns
- Consider performance implications

### Logging
Implement unified logging approach

- Use structured logging
- Include relevant context
- Use appropriate log levels
- Configure proper log handlers

### Error Management
Proper error handling with context

- Use specific exception types
- Include error context
- Handle errors at appropriate levels
- Provide meaningful error messages

## AI Friendliness

### Code Structure
- Use clear and logical file organization
- Keep functions focused and well-named
- Use descriptive variable names
- Add comments for complex logic

### Type System
- Use type hints consistently
- Keep type annotations readable
- Avoid overly complex type constructs
- Document type usage where needed

### Documentation
- Write clear and concise docstrings
- Include examples in documentation
- Explain non-obvious decisions
- Keep documentation up to date

### Testing
- Write clear test cases
- Include test documentation
- Make tests easy to understand
- Test edge cases explicitly

## Validation Requirements
- Code must follow all required guidelines
- Documentation must be complete and clear
- Tests must cover all functionality
- Code must be AI-friendly and maintainable 